Why is a tree appropriate for the doctor structure?
When might a software engineer use preorder, inorder, or postorder traversals?
How do heaps help simulate real-time systems liek emergency intake?

I think a tree makes sense for the doctor structure because a hospital is
basically one big chain of command. Every doctor reports to one manager, and
some of them have doctors under them. In my DoctorTree, Dr. Croft is the root,
and Dr. Phan and Dr. Goldsmith branch off below. Since every doctor has just
one path back to the top, it's easy to see who reports to who. I also added
checks in insert for duplicates, empty names, and managers that don't exist,
so the hierarchy doesn't get messed up.

Each traversal is useful for something different. Preorder visits a manager
before their team, so it works well for printing out an org chart. Inorder
goes left, then the node, then right, which gives sorted data if you're using
a binary search tree. Postorder visits the subordinates first and the manager
last, which would help for something like deleting a tree or adding up a
department's workload before reporting it upward. In my program, postorder
prints Dr. Morgan and Dr. Carson before Dr. Phan, and Dr. Croft comes last.

Heaps are great for emergency intake because the sickest patient needs to be
seen first, no matter when they showed up. My MinHeap sorts Patient objects by
urgency, where a lower number means higher priority. Insert and remove_min run
in O(log n) time, and peek is O(1). In my test, Taylor (urgency 1) came in
after Jordan (urgency 3) but still got served first. With a regular list,
you'd have to keep sorting or searching every time, but a heap stays fast even
as more patients keep coming in.